window.MONSTER_CHUNK_121 = [{"name":"Stunjelly","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"ooze","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"blindsight","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":9,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":9,"ac_mods":"-1 size, +0 natural","hp":42,"hd":"4d8+24","hdvalue":4,"fort":7,"ref":1,"will":-4,"defensive_abilities":["camouflage"],"immunities":["ooze traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":10}],"melee":"slam +3 (1d6+1 plus 1d6 acid plus paralysis)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"corrosion"},{"type":"engulf","details":"DC 13, 1d6 acid and paralysis"},{"type":"paralysis","details":"3d6 rounds, DC 18"}],"strength":12,"dexterity":10,"constitution":22,"wisdom":1,"charisma":1,"baseatk":3,"cmb":5,"cmd":15,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"What appears to be a section of the wall comes to life, revealing a slightly translucent protoplasmic creature.","group":"Jelly","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Acid","kind":"Ex","details":"A stunjelly secretes a digestive acid that dissolves organic material quickly, but doesn't affect metal or stone. Any melee hit deals acid damage, and the opponent's clothing and armor (non-metal only) take the same amount of damage unless they succeed on DC 18 Reflex save. A wooden weapon that strikes a stunjelly takes 1d6 acid damage unless it succeeds on a DC 18 Reflex save. If a stunjelly remains in contact with a wooden object for 1 full round, it inflicts 18 points of damage (no save) to the object. The save DCs are Constitution-based."},{"type":"Camouflage","kind":"Ex","details":"Since a stunjelly looks like normal stone when at rest, it takes a DC 20 Perception check to notice it before it attacks. Anyone with ranks in Survival or Knowledge (dungeoneering) can use one of those skills instead of Perception to notice the stunjelly. Dwarves can use stonecunning to notice a stunjelly."},{"type":"Corrosion","kind":"Ex","details":"An opponent that is being engulfed by a stunjelly suffers a -4 penalty on Reflex saves made to resist acid damage applying to clothing and armor."}],"description":"Stunjellies are distant relatives of the dungeon-dwelling gelatinous cube. It appears as a section of ordinary wall and covers an area of at least 10 square ft., and is 2-1/2 to 5 ft. thick. A stunjelly is gray in color and slightly translucent. It gives off a mild vinegar odor that can be detected at a range of 5 ft. (DC 15 Perception check). When a target moves within 5 ft. of a stunjelly, it attacks with its slam attack. Any creature paralyzed by a stunjelly is engulfed and devoured.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Stunjelly originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Sudoth","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"aberration","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"all-around vision"},{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"watersense","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +3 natural","hp":26,"hd":"4d8+8","hdvalue":4,"fort":3,"ref":4,"will":5,"dr":"5/slashing or piercing","weaknesses":["Water dependant"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":5},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"2 slam +7 (1d6+4), spear +7 (1d8+4), tentacle +5 (1d8+2 plus paralysis)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"paralysis","details":"1d6 rounds, DC 14"}],"strength":18,"dexterity":16,"constitution":14,"intelligence":11,"wisdom":12,"charisma":8,"baseatk":3,"cmb":7,"cmd":20,"feats":[{"feat":"Multiattack"},{"feat":"Swim-By Attack"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":12},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10},{"skill":"Survival","value":8},{"skill":"Swim","value":19}],"environments":["temperate aquatic","warm aquatic"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-4)","school (5-8)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This vaguely humanoid creature has a head that resembles a large jelly fish. Extending down from its large head is a manlike torso and arms that again change into a long knot of feathery writhing tentacles which it uses as a means of propulsion beneath the waves. Its lower torso is a mass of writhing and twitching tentacles.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Water Dependent","kind":"Ex","details":"A sudoth can survive out of the water for 1 hour per 2 points of Constitution (after that, see to the Drowning)."},{"type":"Watersense","kind":"Ex","details":"A sudoth can automatically pinpoint anything in contact with the water within 60 ft."}],"description":"Sudoth are a curious species thought to be the offspring of sailors stranded in windless seas and some cursed horror of the deep. For this reason they are often attacked by mariners on sight. In truth, the sudoth are a peaceable if dangerous species that trade freely between locathah, sahaugin, and occasionally sea elves and merfolk. Their pliable flesh proves immune to the pressures of deep waters, allowing them to salvage riches from the depths that even other races of the seas find impossible to retrieve. They are enemies of krakens and aboleth who find them a delicious tasting delicacy. Sudoths make their lairs deep under the oceans and sustain themselves on a diet of small fish and plant life. Hunters by nature, they often employ spears and nets when on a hunting excursion. The fleshy parts of this creature vary in color from a milky white transparent color to a deep, rich bluish-green. Its tentacles range in color from gray to dark blue and vary in size with the sudoth (the larger specimen's tentacles reach lengths of 10 ft. or more). A small almost unnoticeable band of eyes (the same color as its flesh) ring the circumference of its head. Sudoth attack with weapons such as spears or with slam attacks. Their tail tentacles excrete a paralytic substance. A common tactic is to grapple and paralyze a foe with its tentacles, and finish them off with stabs from their spear. Its malleable form protects it against most bludgeoning attacks; thus opponents are advised to employ piercing or slashing weapons when combating a sudoth.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Swamp Troll","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CE","size":"Large","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["giant"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +5 natural, -1 size","hp":38,"hd":"4d8+20","hdvalue":4,"hp_mods":["regeneration 3 (acid or fire)"],"fort":9,"ref":3,"will":2,"weaknesses":["swamp dependent"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +6 (2d6+4), 2 claws +6 (1d6+4)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"10 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rend","details":"2 claws, 1d6+6"}],"strength":18,"dexterity":14,"constitution":20,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":9,"charisma":4,"baseatk":3,"cmb":8,"cmd":20,"feats":[{"feat":"Improved Natural Attack (bite)","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Iron Will"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":3},{"skill":"Stealth","value":4,"special":"+8 swampy or forested areas"},{"skill":"Survival","value":3},{"skill":"Swim","value":12}],"languages":["Giant"],"environments":["temperate marshes"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-4)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This large hulking brute has long, thick arms and legs, both of which end in sharpened and filthy claws. Its body is covered with moss and fungus, and its hair is dark brownish-green. Large, upward-curving fangs jut from its lower jaw.","group":"Troll","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Swamp Dependent","kind":"Ex","details":"Swamp trolls keep their bodies covered in a thick coating of mud and swamp water. Without such a coating, they lose their regeneration ability and eventually suffocate. They can survive away from their murky home for 1 hour per 2 points of Constitution. After that, see the Suffocation. They cannot regenerate while subject to suffocation from being away from their swamp for too long."}],"description":"Swamp trolls are large, stocky, dark gray or brown hunched humanoids. Their flesh is slick and slimy like moss. Swamp trolls make their lairs deep in swampland and marshes away from more settled areas, but not far enough away where they cannot hunt humans if game and other food runs scarce in the swamps. Swamp trolls are 7-foot tall hunched humanoids and weigh about 400 pounds. Swamp trolls are aggressive predators that attack living creatures on sight (especially when a swamp troll is hungry). When hunting a swamp troll moves quietly along, easing closer to its prey and then finally striking with its claws and bite when within range. Swamp trolls rarely fight to the death unless threatened or hungry.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Swordtooth Shark","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"animal","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":5,"senses":[{"type":"blindsense","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"keen scent"},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +6 natural,-1 size","hp":39,"hd":"6d8+12","hdvalue":6,"fort":9,"ref":6,"will":3,"speed":[{"type":"swim","speed":60}],"melee":"bite +8 (2d6+8/18-20 plus bleed)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"bleed","details":"1d6, DC 15"}],"strength":19,"dexterity":12,"constitution":15,"intelligence":1,"wisdom":12,"charisma":2,"baseatk":4,"cmb":9,"cmd":20,"feats":[{"feat":"Great Fortitude"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Swim","value":12}],"environments":["any oceans","any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","school (2-5)","pack (6-9)"],"treasure":"incidental","description_visual":"This giant gray shark opens its great mouth to reveal multiple rows of dagger-like teeth.","group":"Shark","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Keen Scent","kind":"Ex","details":"A swordtooth shark can notice creatures by scent in a 180-foot radius underwater and can detect blood in the water at ranges of up to a mile."},{"type":"Savage Bite","kind":"Ex","details":"A swordtooth shark applies twice its Strength bonus to damage dealt with its bite attack, and it threatens a critical hit on an 18-20."}],"description":"Swordtooth sharks are highly aggressive carnivores that spend most of their time hunting and killing. Swordtooth sharks are found in both freshwater and saltwater, and while most dwell in the large expanse of the world's oceans, some take to underground rivers and lakes where the fish are plentiful and other predators are rare. Swordtooth sharks, like their brethren, are pure killing machines, highly aggressive when hungry, and extremely territorial. A typical swordtooth's territory can cover several square miles.\n\nSwordtooths, like some other sharks, are not required to be in perpetual motion. They often rest on the bottom of the ocean's floors staying motionless for up to 24 hours. When resting, a swordtooth is highly attuned to its surroundings and always has its eyes open and focused on anything moving near it. Because of their resting habits, some swordtooths have developed ambush tactics, lying in wait on the ocean floor for prey to swim nearby before striking with blinding speed.\n\nThe average swordtooth shark is 10 ft. long and weighs 350 pounds, but can grow to lengths of up to 20 ft. and weigh over 500 pounds. A swordtooth shark has three rows of razor-sharp teeth, pointed lower teeth for gripping its prey and serrated upper teeth for tearing flesh and muscle. Smaller prey is usually swallowed whole, while large prey is torn into large chunks before being eaten. Occasionally a swordtooth is cut open and a small amount of treasure spills out; remnants of a previously digested meal. A swordtooth has an average lifespan of 20-30 years while those that have migrated underground have an average lifespan of roughly 20-25 years.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Tangtal","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"magical beast","init":4,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision","modifier":"null"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +3 natural","hp":22,"hd":"3d6+10","hdvalue":3,"fort":5,"ref":7,"will":15,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40}],"melee":"bite +7 (1d6+3), 2 claws +7 (1d4+3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":16,"dexterity":19,"constitution":15,"intelligence":12,"wisdom":13,"charisma":10,"baseatk":3,"cmb":33,"cmd":43,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":8},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9}],"languages":["Common","Sylvan"],"sq":[{"type":"duplicate"}],"environments":["temperate forests","temperate plains"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"Small white flecks cover its head, throat and neck, and shoulders, and its long, upward curving tail ends with a white tip. It stands on muscular legs with huge paws with sharpened claws.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Duplicate","kind":"Su","details":"As a standard action, a tangtal can create an effect similar to a mirror image spell (caster level 12th). This allows the tangtal to create up to eight images of itself. The images can move up to 30 ft. away from the tangtal, but each must be within 10 ft. of another image. The tangtal can use this ability once per day and it otherwise resembles the mirror image spell."}],"description":"Tangtals, or dupli-cats, are ferocious and intelligent panthers with the ability to produce illusory images of themselves. They roam haunted woodlands or plains and often lair near civilized areas.\n\nTangtals are solitary creatures; no recorded encounter has ever placed more than one of these creatures together. Mating season is the only time more than one of these creatures is likely to be encountered and sages have argued as to exactly when tangtals mate. Tangtal young are unknown and none have ever been seen.\n\nTangtals are carnivores and prefer to dine on small animals such as rats, moles, squirrels, and so on. In such instances when food is scarce, it isn't unheard of for a tangtal to sneak into a civilized area and carry off or kill chickens, hens, dogs, cats, and even small children. A tangtal is about 7 ft. long from nose to tail and weighs about 350 to 400 pounds.\n\nTangtals wait for prey to come within range before leaping to attack with their wicked bite and sharpened claws. They use their duplicate ability as a defense mechanism against larger or stronger foes, or when they're outnumbered. Tangtals are smart enough to know when the day is lost, and if combat goes against them, they flee.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Tether","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"LE","size":"Small","type":"magical beast","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":14,"ac_flat_footed":12,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +1 natural, +1 size","hp":26,"hd":"4d10+4","hdvalue":4,"fort":5,"ref":7,"will":7,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"climb","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +8 (1d4-1), 2 claws +8 (1d3-1)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"energy drain","details":"1 level, DC 14"},{"type":"fascination"}],"strength":8,"dexterity":16,"constitution":12,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":14,"charisma":14,"baseatk":4,"cmb":21,"cmd":35,"feats":[{"feat":"Iron Will"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":7,"special":"+15 when balancing"},{"skill":"Climb","value":11},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":5},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":15}],"environments":["any land"],"organizations":["solitary"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature has the appearance of an emaciated cat with wiry gray fur. Its eyes are bright emerald green and its tail is unusually long.","group":"Graymalkin","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Energy Drain","kind":"Su","details":"Each hour a tether remains within 20 ft. of a creature it has fascinated, it deals one negative level to him or her."},{"type":"Fascination","kind":"Su","details":"Will DC 14."}],"description":"Tethers are generally friendly (at least they act that way) to most creatures they encounter. When it selects an owner, it follows that creature around purring and meowing. If it manages to kill its owner with its energy drain ability, it selects a new owner from the remaining family members (if any). A tether is more outgoing than its relative, the slinker, and enjoys crawling into its owner's lap, wrapping its tail around its owner's legs or arm and gently purring. Of course, the entire time this is going on, it is draining its owner's life force.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Thorny","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"plant","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +5 natural","hp":26,"hd":"4d8+8","hdvalue":4,"fort":6,"ref":3,"will":4,"immunities":["plant traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":50}],"melee":"bite +5 (1d6+3 plus trip)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"thorns"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":15,"constitution":14,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":12,"charisma":6,"baseatk":3,"cmb":2,"cmd":16,"feats":[{"feat":"Combat Reflexes"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":13},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11,"special":"+19 in swampy or forested areas"}],"environments":["warm forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (3-8)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature resembles a hunting dog constructed of tangled briars, vines, leaves, and sticks. Its entire body is covered with small, sharp thorns.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Thorns","kind":"Ex","details":"A thorny's body is covered with sharp wooden thorns. Any creature attacking a thorny with a non-reach melee weapon or with natural attacks takes 1d4+2 points of piercing damage from the thorny's sharp thorns. A creature that grapples with a thorny takes this same damage each round."}],"description":"Thornies are the pets and companions of the vegepygmies. They are rarely encountered outside of a vegepygmy tribe or hunting party.\n\nVegepygmies often use them to hunt prey.\n\nThornies reproduce by planting egg-like seeds (that the female lays) in the ground. Three to six months later, a small tree sprouts, buds, and from these buds are born the thornies. An average thorny tree is capable of producing 1d6+4 thornies. The tree dies once it buds and the thornies \"hatch.\"","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Thorny originally appeared in the First Edition module S3 Expedition to the Barrier Peaks (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1980) and later in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Tsathar Scourge","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CE","size":"Medium","type":"monstrous humanoid","subtypes":["aquatic"],"init":6,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"90 ft."},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+2 armor, +2 Dex, +4 natural","hp":33,"hd":"4d10+8 (+4)","hdvalue":4,"fort":3,"ref":6,"will":29,"defensive_abilities":["slimy"],"weaknesses":["light blindness"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"swim","speed":30}],"melee":"shortspear +6 (1d6+2) or kukri +6 (1d4+2/18-20) or 2 claws +6 (1d6+2); and bite +6 (1d4+2)","ranged":"shortspear +6 (1d6+2) or net +6 touch (entangled)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"leap"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":14,"constitution":14,"intelligence":12,"wisdom":14,"charisma":12,"baseatk":4,"cmb":6,"cmd":18,"cmd_special":"+12 vs. grapple","feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)","type":"bonus"},{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Toughness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":3,"special":"+17 long jumping, +27 high jumping"},{"skill":"Climb","value":6},{"skill":"Escape Artist","value":15},{"skill":"Handle Animal","value":7,"special":"+15 with amphibians"},{"skill":"Perception","value":11},{"skill":"Stealth","value":8},{"skill":"Swim","value":16}],"languages":["Abyssal","Tsathar"],"sq":[{"type":"amphibious"},{"type":"implant","details":"DC 21"}],"environments":["temperate marshes","any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-5)","pack (6-40 tsathar, with 1-4 tsathar scourges accompanied by 3-12 killer monstrous frogs, led by a tsathar cleric or oracle of 3rd-6th level)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This vile creature resembles an upright, humanoid frog with gray flesh and reddish-gold eyes. Its humanoid arms end in wicked claws.","group":"Tsathar","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"A tsathar scourge is a slightly bulkier tsathar, of equivalent height but weighing about 350 pounds. Tsathar Scourge Society Tsathar scourges hold a special place of honor in their society, as they serve as breeders of the killer monstrous frogs (q.v.) which commonly accompany them as pets and guardians, and by extension oversee the breeding of the tsathar themselves. They wear a badge of station that indicates their control over the various beasts. All frogs bred by the tsathar obey scourges. Tsathar scourges never become priests and thus don't have the summoning ability common tsathars can attain. It is also rumored that tsathar scourges are created when a common tsathar consumes an implanted egg of a tsathar destined for the priest class in the hours before it hatches.","as_character":[{"description":"Tsathar scourges also favor barbarian, ranger, rogue, and fighter classes.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Strength, +4 Dexterity, +4 Constitution, +2 Intelligence, +4 Wisdom, and +2 Charisma."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"Tsathar can see in the dark up to 90 ft. Scent."},{"type":"Racial Hit Dice","value":"Tsathar scourges begin with four levels of monstrous humanoid, providing 4d10 Hit dice, a base attack bonus of +4, and base saving throw bonuses of Fort +1, Ref +4, and Will +4."},{"type":"Racial Skills","value":"A tsathar scourge's monstrous humanoid levels give it skill points equal to 4 x (4 + Int modifier) for scourges. Scourges have Handle Animal as a class skill. Tsathar scourges gain a +12 racial bonus on Escape Artist checks from their slimy ability, a +8 racial bonus to Swim checks from their Swim speed, a +14 racial bonus on Acrobatic checks when making long jumps, and a +24 racial bonus on Acrobatic checks when making high jumps. In addition, tsathar scourges have a +8 racial bonus on Handle Animal checks when dealing with amphibians such as frogs and toads."},{"type":"Racial Feats","value":"A tsathar scourge's monstrous humanoid levels give it two feats. In addition, all tsathar gain Skill Focus (Perception) as a bonus feat."},{"type":"AC","value":"+4 natural armor bonus."},{"type":"Weaknesses","value":"Light blindness."},{"type":"Natural Weapons","value":"Tsathar scourges can fight with 2 claws that deal 1d6 base damage and a bite that deals 1d4 base damage."},{"type":"Weapon Proficiency","value":"Tsathar scourges are automatically proficient with the kukri, net, and shortspear."},{"type":"Special Attacks","value":"Leap."},{"type":"Special Qualities","value":"Amphibious, implant, slimy."},{"type":"Languages","value":"All tsathar begin play speaking Abyssal and Tsathar. Bonus languages for tsathar are Aklo, Common, Draconic, Gnome, Terran, and Undercommon."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Tsathar first appeared in the Necromancer Games adventure D1 Tomb of Abysthor (©2001, Clark Peterson and Bill Webb, Necromancer Games, Inc.) as servants of the demonic frog god Tsathogga.","gear":"leather armor, shortspear, kukri, net"},{"name":"Valeany","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"CN","size":"Medium","type":"fey","init":4,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":18,"ac_touch":15,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+4 Dex, +1 dodge, +3 natural","hp":22,"hd":"4d6+8","hdvalue":4,"fort":3,"ref":8,"will":6,"dr":"5/cold iron","speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"mwk dagger +7 (1d4/19-20) or touch +6 (mark) or unarmed +6 (1d3 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"embrace"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":6,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":8,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"call lightning"},{"spell":"dimension door"},{"spell":"flame blade"},{"spell":"hold person"},{"spell":"summon nature's ally III"},{"spell":"tree shape"}]},{"frequency":"3/day","spells":[{"spell":"entangle"},{"spell":"hypnotism"},{"spell":"suggestion"}]}],"strength":10,"dexterity":19,"constitution":15,"intelligence":15,"wisdom":15,"charisma":19,"baseatk":2,"cmb":2,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":17,"feats":[{"feat":"Dodge"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Bluff","value":11},{"skill":"Diplomacy","value":11},{"skill":"Handle Animal","value":8},{"skill":"Knowledge (nature)","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":11},{"skill":"Survival","value":6}],"languages":["Common","Sylvan"],"sq":[{"type":"nature's empathy"}],"environments":["temperate forests"],"treasure":"standard (masterwork dagger)","description_visual":"An exquisitely beautiful creature with pointed ears and long flowing hair emerges from the forest.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Embrace","kind":"Su","details":"A valeany that grapples and pins a foe deals 1d4 points of Strength damage to the target each round the pin is maintained."},{"type":"Mark","kind":"Su","details":"A valeany can \"mark\" an opponent that instills distrust in all animals. No animal, wild or domesticated, approaches within 30 ft. of a marked foe; if forced to do so, the creature panics and remains so as long as it's within 30 ft. of the target. The mark can be removed by a break enchantment or dispel magic (the latter of which must be cast by a spellcaster whose caster level is equal to or higher than the valeany's Hit Dice). Dryads, druids, and valeanys can sense a marked creature within 60 ft."},{"type":"Nature's Empathy","kind":"Su","details":"This ability functions as a druid's wild empathy class feature, except the valeany has a +4 racial bonus on the check. The valeany's effective druid level is equal to her Hit Dice for determining her total modifier to the check."}],"description":"The valeany or \"forest girl\" is thought to be distantly related to the dryad and nymph. She makes her home deep in a secluded area of a forest, not far from civilized areas perhaps but secluded enough that it is away from prying eyes. Valeanys are free-spirited creatures that enjoy games, frivolity, and nature. They are often found in the company of dryads, nymphs, sirines, and elves.\n\nA valeany appears as a comely human female with long flowing golden hair that nearly reaches the ground. The eyes of a valeany are a deep golden blue or brown. Skin color ranges from light golden to dark tan. The valeany wears either lightly colored and lightweight gowns or nothing at all.\n\nValeany, like their cousins the dryad and nymph, are not combatants. They seek to avoid combat when possible, unless they themselves, their homes, or their allies are threatened or harmed. Most often, a valeany uses its spell-like abilities to slip away from battle as soon as possible, marking its enemies as it does so.\n\nIf forced into combat, a valeany fights using its spell-like abilities and embrace. Incapacitated or weakened foes are left where they fall.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Vampire Rose","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"plant","init":0,"senses":[{"type":"blindsight","modifier":"30 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":17,"ac_mods":"+6 natural, +1 size","hp":30,"hd":"4d8+12","hdvalue":4,"fort":7,"ref":1,"will":2,"defensive_abilities":["camouflage"],"immunities":["plant traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":5}],"melee":"stalk +6 (1d4+2 plus grab)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft. (10 ft. with stalk)","special_attacks":[{"type":"blood drain","details":"1d4 Constitution"}],"strength":14,"dexterity":10,"constitution":16,"wisdom":13,"charisma":8,"baseatk":3,"cmb":4,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":16,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","environments":["temperate forests"],"organizations":["Solitary","patch (2-4)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This bush has many flowering white bulbs and petals, green stems lined with tiny thorns, and many small branches of greenish-brown.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Camouflage","kind":"Ex","details":"Since a vampire rose looks like a normal white rose bush when at rest, it takes a DC 20 Perception check to notice it before it attacks. Anyone with ranks in Survival or Knowledge (nature) can use one of those skills instead of Perception to notice the plant. Dwarves can use stonecunning to notice the subterranean version."}],"description":"Vampire roses look like normal white rose bushes and are often mistaken for such. The typical vampire rose bush stands about 3 ft. tall.\n\nA vampire rose stands motionless until its prey moves within range, when it strikes with a thorny stalk. Opponents are grabbed and drained of blood. When fully sated with blood, a vampire rose's petals flush red.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Vampire Rose originally appeared in the First Edition module (revised edition) B3 Palace of the Silver Princess (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Vegepygmy Bodyguard","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Small","type":"plant","subtypes":["fungus"],"init":2,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":13,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +3 natural, +1 size","hp":37,"hd":"5d8+15","hdvalue":5,"fort":6,"ref":3,"will":2,"dr":"5/bludgeoning or slashing","immunities":["electricity","plant traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"2 claws +6 (1d3+2) or spear +7 (1d6+3 /x3)","ranged":"spear +7 (1d6+2/x3)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":15,"dexterity":14,"constitution":15,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":13,"charisma":12,"baseatk":3,"cmb":4,"cmd":16,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Toughness"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (spear)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":12},{"skill":"Stealth","value":18,"special":"+26 in forested or swampy areas"}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","gang (2-5)","patrol (4-7)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This creature resembles a humanoid with green vegetable-like skin and razor-sharp claws. Its head sports two large yellow eyes, a wide mouth, inset nose, and a topknot of dark brown leaves. Leafy tendrils protrude from its shoulders, midsection, arms, and legs.","group":"Vegepygmy","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","description":"When a living creature is slain by russet mold (see that entry), it rises as a vegepygmy commoner. Vegepygmies are low-intelligence plants that make their home deep in the forests or underground away from most settled areas. They are hunters and scavengers, and carnivorous, preying on living creatures weaker than themselves. A vegepygmy ranges from two ft. to four and a half ft. tall. Though they don't seem to have ears, it is well known that vegepygmies can hear. Vegepygmies don't speak, but communicate with others of their kind by thumping their chest or rapping their spears on rocks, earth, or some other solid surface. It isn't known if or how they communicate with other creatures. Vegepygmies attack with claws or weapons, usually either a longspear or club. They prefer to ambush opponents, using their coloration to blend with their natural surroundings.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Vegepygmy originally appeared in the First Edition module S3 Expedition to the Barrier Peaks (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1980) and later in the First Edition Monster Manual II (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1983) and is used by permission.","gear":"spear"},{"name":"Velvet Ant Swarm","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"N","size":"Diminutive","type":"vermin","subtypes":["swarm"],"init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":17,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +4 size, +0 natural","hp":27,"hd":"5d8+5","hdvalue":5,"fort":5,"ref":4,"will":1,"immunities":["mind-affecting effects","weapon damage","swarm traits","vermin traits"],"weaknesses":["swarm traits"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30},{"type":"climb","speed":20}],"melee":"swarm (1d6 plus poison)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"distraction","details":"DC 13"}],"strength":1,"dexterity":16,"constitution":13,"wisdom":11,"charisma":4,"baseatk":3,"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":11}],"environments":["warm forests","warm plains"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-4 swarms)","cluster (6-11 swarms)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"A moving carpet of big, red ants with black heads sweeps towards you.","group":"Velvet Ant","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Swarm-injury; save Fort DC 13; frequency 1/ round for 4 rounds; effect 1 Dexterity damage; cure 1 save. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"Velvet ants are not really ants at all but a form of wasp. The females are wingless while the males have wings. Velvet ants have a bright red abdomen and thorax and a black head and black legs. Its thorax and abdomen are covered with short, coarse, red hairs. Typical adults reach a length of 1 inch.\n\nVelvet ants feed on the nectar of flowers, though on some occasions, the adults have been known to feed on other wasps and bees, particularly bumblebees or honey bees. Other such insects are generally left alone.\n\nVelvet ants typically make their lair in bee hives or nests they have taken over. Generally an assault is short and quick, with the velvet ants swarming the bees and quickly gaining the upper hand, slaying all bees in a single nest. Slain bees are kept as food for the velvet ant larvae.\n\nVelvet ants are generally inoffensive. Most encounters are with a swarm moving from one location to another, and most attacks happen when a careless individual wanders into the swarm. Male velvet ants are completely harmless. Females compose any swarm encountered and they attack with a painful sting.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Wang Liang","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"LE","size":"Medium","type":"outsider","subtypes":["evil","extraplanar","lawful"],"init":6,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"ac":15,"ac_touch":12,"ac_flat_footed":13,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +3 natural","hp":26,"hd":"4d10+4","hdvalue":4,"hp_mods":["fast healing 2"],"fort":2,"ref":6,"will":6,"dr":"5/silver","immunities":["poison"],"resists":[{"type":"fire","value":5}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":30}],"melee":"bite +6 (1d6+1 plus poison), 2 claws +6 (1d4+1 plus poison)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","spell_like_abilities_caster_level":4,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":5,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"detect good"}]},{"frequency":"3/day","spells":[{"spell":"curse water"},{"spell":"darkness"}]}],"strength":12,"dexterity":15,"constitution":13,"intelligence":10,"wisdom":14,"charisma":12,"baseatk":4,"cmb":5,"cmd":17,"feats":[{"feat":"Improved Initiative"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Bluff","value":8},{"skill":"Climb","value":8},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":6},{"skill":"Knowledge (planes)","value":5},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Sense Motive","value":9},{"skill":"Survival","value":9}],"languages":["Common","Infernal"],"environments":["any lawful evil-aligned plane"],"organizations":["solitary","troupe (3-7)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This feral humanoid creature is completely hairless with gray skin. Its eyes are pools of midnight with glittering red ruby dots that serve as pupils and shine with a hellish glow. Its hands end in razor-sharpened talons that have the appearance and color of iron. Its mouth is lined with rows of shark-like teeth and its ears are pointed.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Bite, Claw-injury; save Fort DC 12; frequency 1/ round for 2 rounds; effect 1d4 Str; cure 1 save. The save DC is Constitution-based."}],"description":"The wang liang are an infernal race of humanoid beings bred for power and cruelty by the authority of Lucifer himself. It is said that the blood of Lucifer himself and that of his most trusted advisors was mixed with that of mortal races to spawn this horrid hybrid. Before his fall, the wang liang were the former ruler of Hell's most devout worshippers. The wang liang organized themselves into monk-like sects and frequently acted as spies and enforcers in Hell and as emissaries and advisors to mortal rulers across the material planes. A typical wang liang stands 6 ft. tall and weighs 200 pounds. It speaks Infernal and Common. At will the wang liang may cause their iron clawed fingertips and razor sharp fangs to drip with an infernal venom. Wang liang prefer to use misdirection and intimidation to alter the outcome of fights and bide their time if possible to assault foes when they are at their most vulnerable. It isn't uncommon for a wang liang to use allies to their advantage. When pressed into actual combat they use full attacks in an attempt to infuse their opponent with as much venom as possible. Wang liang masters are often found leading monasteries of wang liang monks dedicated to Lucifer. Such monasteries dedicated to the Dark One are located in remote, out of the way places or cleverly disguised as another business or monastery if found within a populated area. Wang liang were once greater than they currently are, having suffered great losses and in many cases a thinning of their infernal bloodline in the course of their masters defeat. Spiteful over their diminished authority, they now pursue strength through exercise of their old monastic ways and practice of martial arts or pursuit of sorcerous knowledge. The wang liang still organize themselves into regimented monastic orders, sending their most gifted champions out on missions to spread the words of Lucifer.","as_character":[{"description":"Wang liang leaders are usually monks or monk/sorcerers. Having been bred in the lusts of Lucifer and with the industry of the coal chambers of hell the wang liang player characters are often raised within the monasteries of lawful evil sects of monks upon the material planes. Due to their natural attacks and nerve numbing venom they quickly rise in the ranks of such sanctuaries of hellish intent, often serving as grand champions and grand masters. In Infernus, entire legions of wang liang monks are at Lucifer's immediate command, with several masters serving as elite bodyguards. Wang liang characters possess the following racial traits.","racial_traits":[{"type":"Ability Score Bonuses","value":"+2 Strength, +4 Dexterity, +2 Constitution, +4 Wisdom, +2 Charisma: Wang liang are superior in mind and body."},{"type":"Darkvision","value":"A wang liang can see in darkness to a range of 60 ft."},{"type":"Racial Hit Dice","value":"A wang liang begins with four levels of outsider, which provide 4d10 Hit Dice, a base attack bonus of +4, and base saving throw bonuses of Fort +1, Ref +3, and Will +3."},{"type":"Racial Skills","value":"A wang liang's outsider levels give it skill points equal to 4 x (6 + Int modifier, minimum 1). Climb, Intimidate, Knowledge (religion) and Survival are class skill for wang liang."},{"type":"Racial Feats","value":"A wang liang's outsider levels give it two feats."},{"type":"AC","value":"+3 natural armor bonus."},{"type":"Fast Healing","value":"Wang liang possess fast healing 2."},{"type":"Defensive Abilities","value":"Wang liang gain DR 5/silver and resist fire 5 Immunities: Wang liang are immune to poison"},{"type":"Natural Weapons","value":"Wang liang can fight with a bite (1d6) and two claws (1d4)."},{"type":"Special Attacks","value":"Poison."},{"type":"Spell-like abilities","value":"A wang liang can cast detect good at will, and curse water and darkness each 3 times per day, using its levels in outsider as its caster level."},{"type":"Languages","value":"Wang liang begin play speaking Infernal. Those with an Intelligence score of at least 10 also speak Common. Wang liangs with high Intelligence scores can choose from the following bonus languages: Abyssal, Celestial, Draconic, and Sylvan."}]}],"ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Werewolverine","cr":3,"xp":800,"race":"Human","classes":[{"class":"barbarian","level":3}],"alignment":"CN","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["human","shapechanger"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":13,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":11,"ac_mods":"+3 armor, +1 Dex, -2 rage, +1 shield","hp":43,"hd":"3d12+12 (+6)","hdvalue":3,"fort":7,"ref":2,"will":4,"defensive_abilities":["uncanny dodge"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40}],"melee":"battleaxe +8 (1d8+5/x3)","ranged":"throwing axe +4 (1d6+5)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rage","details":"10 rounds"},{"type":"rage power","details":"intimidating glare"}],"strength":21,"dexterity":13,"constitution":18,"intelligence":8,"wisdom":12,"charisma":10,"baseatk":3,"cmb":8,"cmd":19,"feats":[{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Intimidate)"},{"feat":"Toughness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":9},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Survival","value":7}],"languages":["Common"],"sq":[{"type":"change shape","details":"human, hybrid, and wolverine; polymorph"},{"type":"fast movement"},{"type":"trap sense","details":"+1"},{"type":"lycanthropic empathy","details":"badgers and wolverines"}],"environments":["cold forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","family (3-6)","pack (6-10)","troupe (11-20)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Howling out of the taiga comes a ferocious humanoid creature covered in thick grey fur. Its hands are tipped in long claws.","group":"Lycanthrope","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","description":"In their humanoid forms, werewolverines are wiry, muscular folk with broad shoulders and powerful hands. Their faces are almost always contorted into a twitchy sneer. Quick to anger and slow to calm, werewolverines are seemingly always on the brink of a powerful emotional outburst. When angered, a werewolverine screams and howls in a raspy voice that speaks of violent, primal rage.\n\nInstinctively brutal, werewolverines make the ideal strong-arms for bandit gangs.\n\nNote that the statistics presented here for werewolverines assume that the creatures are in their barbarian rage - if the creatures are encountered at another time, simply adjust their statistics accordingly.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"Original author Nathan Douglas Paul  Originally appearing in The Eamonvale Incursion (© Necromancer Games, 2008)","gear":"battleaxe, 2 daggers, 2 throwing axes, studded leather armor, small wooden shield, other gear"},{"name":"Werewolverine","cr":3,"xp":800,"race":"Human","classes":[{"class":"barbarian","level":3}],"alignment":"CN","size":"Medium","type":"humanoid","subtypes":["human","shapechanger"],"init":1,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"},{"type":"scent"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+3 armor, +1 Dex, +4 natural, -2 rage, +1 shield","hp":46,"hd":"3d12+15 (+6)","hdvalue":3,"fort":8,"ref":2,"will":4,"defensive_abilities":["uncanny dodge"],"dr":"10/ silver","speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40}],"melee":"battleaxe +9 (1d8+6/x3), or 2 claws +9 (1d6+6), bite +9 (1d4+6 plus curse of lycanthropy; DC 15)","ranged":"throwing axe +4 (1d6+6)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"rage","details":"10 rounds"},{"type":"rage power","details":"intimidating glare"}],"strength":23,"dexterity":13,"constitution":20,"intelligence":8,"wisdom":12,"charisma":10,"baseatk":3,"cmb":8,"cmd":19,"feats":[{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Intimidate)"},{"feat":"Toughness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Climb","value":12},{"skill":"Intimidate","value":9},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Survival","value":7}],"languages":["Common"],"sq":[{"type":"change shape","details":"human, hybrid, and wolverine; polymorph"},{"type":"fast movement"},{"type":"trap sense","details":"+1"},{"type":"lycanthropic empathy","details":"badgers and wolverines"}],"environments":["cold forests"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","family (3-6)","pack (6-10)","troupe (11-20)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"Howling out of the taiga comes a ferocious humanoid creature covered in thick grey fur. Its hands are tipped in long claws.","group":"Lycanthrope","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Rage","kind":"Ex","details":"A werewolverine in hybrid or animal form that takes damage in combat flies into a rage on its next turn, clawing and biting madly until either it or its opponent is dead. The creature cannot end its rage voluntarily."}],"description":"In their humanoid forms, werewolverines are wiry, muscular folk with broad shoulders and powerful hands. Their faces are almost always contorted into a twitchy sneer. Quick to anger and slow to calm, werewolverines are seemingly always on the brink of a powerful emotional outburst. When angered, a werewolverine screams and howls in a raspy voice that speaks of violent, primal rage.\n\nInstinctively brutal, werewolverines make the ideal strong-arms for bandit gangs.\n\nNote that the statistics presented here for werewolverines assume that the creatures are in their barbarian rage - if the creatures are encountered at another time, simply adjust their statistics accordingly.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"Original author Nathan Douglas Paul  Originally appearing in The Eamonvale Incursion (© Necromancer Games, 2008)","gear":"battleaxe, 2 daggers, 2 throwing axes, studded leather armor, small wooden shield, other gear"},{"name":"Wichtlein","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"NG","size":"Tiny","type":"fey","init":3,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":15,"ac_flat_footed":14,"ac_mods":"+3 Dex, +2 natural, +2 size","hp":19,"hd":"3d6+9","hdvalue":3,"fort":4,"ref":6,"will":5,"dr":"5/cold iron","sr":15,"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"speed_mod":"earth glide","melee":"pickaxe +6 (1d3) or touch +6 (slow)","space":"2-1/2 ft.","reach":"0 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"fear"},{"type":"slowing touch","details":"as the slow spell, CL 5th; DC 13 Will"}],"strength":10,"dexterity":16,"constitution":17,"intelligence":9,"wisdom":14,"charisma":8,"baseatk":1,"cmb":2,"cmd":15,"feats":[{"feat":"Alertness"},{"feat":"Weapon Finesse"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Knowledge (dungeoneering)","value":3},{"skill":"Perception","value":10},{"skill":"Profession (miner)","value":5},{"skill":"Stealth","value":17,"special":"+25 underground"},{"skill":"Survival","value":5}],"languages":["Sylvan"],"sq":[{"type":"limited precognition"},{"type":"stonecunning"}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","gang (2-4)","team (5-25)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"The creature sliding out of the stone appears to be a tiny man with ugly, bulbous features, dull gray skin, and gemstone teeth. It is clad in dirty overalls and wears a floppy cap on its head.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Fear","kind":"Su","details":"By scrunching up its face, a wichtlein causes fear in those within 30 ft. that look upon it. Creatures affected must succeed on a DC 10 Will save or be affected by fear (CL 5th). Creatures that save are unaffected by the same wichtlein's fear for one day. Creatures with 6 or more HD are not affected by this fear. The save is Charisma-based."},{"type":"Limited Precognition","kind":"Su","details":"Wichtlein are immediately aware of upcoming danger, so long as the hazard isn't deliberately created by an intelligent creature. Thus, a wichtlein could sense a coming cave-in, or the approach of a bestial monster, but not a raiding party of drow or a miner planning a murder. A wichtlein can sense this danger 3d10 minutes before it occurs, and always attempts to warn any other intelligent creatures within the area."},{"type":"Earth Glide","kind":"Ex","details":"A wichtlein can move through any sort of natural earth or stone as easily as a fish swims through water. It leaves no sign of its passage or hint at its presence to creatures that don't possess tremorsense. A move earth spell cast on an area containing a wichtlein moves the witchlein back 30 ft., stunning the creature for 1 round unless it succeeds on a DC 15 Fortitude save. A wichtlein moving through stone cannot pick up or carry anything it finds within the stone; thus, they still spend a great deal of their time mining."}],"description":"Wichtlein are a race of shy yet helpful fey who primarily dwell in mines, caves, and other underground locations. Although they prefer not to be seen, they are well known for warning miners and explorers of coming dangers, such as cave-ins or approaching monsters. Some communities believe the wichtlein are bad omens, actually causing the disasters about which they warn others, but this is just a myth.\n\nWichtlein are about the size of small halflings, and are occasionally mistaken for such until the viewer draws near enough to recognize how truly ugly the wichtlein is.\n\nUnfortunately while helpful, wichtlein are not especially bright, and usually fail to accurately convey the sort of danger approaching. Most of the time, wichtlein simply pound on nearby rock and stone to indicate danger, a sign that miners are wise to heed.\n\nIndividual wichtlein occasionally leave their mines to dwell in and under the homes of humans or other humanoids. This happens when a wichtlein becomes smitten with a human of the opposite gender, something which occurs rather more frequently than might be expected. These \"house wichtlein\" maintain the home, cleaning and repairing to the best of their ability while their beloved is away. Such \"relationships\" most frequently end when the wichtlein finally reveals itself to the object of its affections, and is usually rejected. Some wichtlein, despite their generally good alignment, wreak havoc on the home of the one who rejected it before returning, dejected, to its mine.\n\nWichtlein dislike confrontation, and prefer to flee when possible. If they have no opportunity to run, and cannot create such an opportunity with their slow and fear abilities, they try to bribe their attackers with knowledge of the local mines and caves. (Wichtlein almost always know where to find gems and valuable metals.) Only if all else fails do they engage in combat.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Zombyre","cr":3,"xp":800,"alignment":"NE","size":"Medium","type":"undead","subtypes":["aquatic","extraplanar"],"init":-1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."}],"auras":["nausea (10 ft., DC 14)"],"ac":15,"ac_touch":9,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"-1 Dex, +6 natural","hp":37,"hd":"5d8+10 (+5)","hdvalue":5,"fort":3,"ref":0,"will":7,"immunities":["undead traits","effects of the River Styx"],"resists":[{"type":"fire","value":20}],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to electricity"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"swim","speed":60}],"melee":"slam +6 (1d8+3 plus grab), bite +6 (1d6+3 plus disease)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":17,"dexterity":8,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":12,"charisma":14,"baseatk":3,"cmb":6,"cmb_special":"+4 vs. grapple","cmd":15,"feats":[{"feat":"Iron Will"},{"feat":"Power Attack"},{"feat":"Toughness"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":4},{"skill":"Swim","value":16}],"languages":["Common (can't speak)"],"sq":[{"type":"staggered"},{"type":"Stygian healing"}],"environments":["any (River Styx)"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","pack (3-8)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature appears to be a rotting corpse, dressed in tattered and torn clothing. Its form is bloated and drips a constant stream of foul, dark water.","source":"Tome of Horrors 4 (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Aura of Nausea","kind":"Ex","details":"A zombyre exudes a sickly stench of death and decay in a 10-foot radius. Creatures entering or within the area must succeed on a DC 14 Fortitude save or become nauseated for as long as they remain in the area plus 1d4 rounds after. Creatures succeeding on their save are immune to the effects of the aura for one day. The save DC is Charisma-based."},{"type":"Disease","kind":"Ex","details":"Stygian Sickness: Bite; save DC 14 Fort; onset 1day; frequency 1/day; effect 1d4 Intelligence and 1d2 Constitution damage; cure 2 consecutive saves. Anyone who dies while infected rises as a zombyre in 2d6 hours. The save is Charisma-based."},{"type":"Staggered","kind":"Ex","details":"Zombyres have poor reflexes and can only perform a single move action or standard action each round. A zombyre can move up to its speed and attack in the same round as a charge action. This flaw only affects zombyres when on land; while underwater, in combat, a zombyre loses this flaw."},{"type":"Stygian Healing","kind":"Su","details":"A zombyre regains 1 hit point each hour when fully submerged in the River Styx. This healing cannot restore more hit points than the maximum the zombyre possesses and it doesn't restore any lost limbs."}],"description":"A zombyre is a living creature that drowned in the River Styx, reanimated by the magic of the Stygian waters for some unknown purpose. It is a free-willed, semi-intelligent creature appearing much as a normal zombie, though bloated from its time spent underwater.\n\nZombyres tend to dwell in packs, loosely organized but seemingly bound together by some unknown force. Some sages speculate this happens when a group of individuals all drown together, such as a group of sailors who perish when their ship sinks. Zombyres tend to stay within the area where they drowned; sometimes making their lair in the very ship that sank beneath the waves and cost them their life. They don't appear to be bound to the area as some undead are to the place where they died. Zombyres are most active at night, rarely venturing onto land during the day. Even nighttime excursions rarely see a zombyre travel more than several hundred ft. from the water's edge. Creatures that venture too close to a zombyre pack are attacked and dragged into the water, where the zombyre pack feasts and dines on the remnants of those so foolhardy.\n\nIn battle, groups of zombyres are relentless combatants and generally swarm their opponents, attacking with their powerful fists and diseased bite. Large packs of zombyres have been known to climb aboard anchored ships, pull the seamen overboard, and attack them in the Stygian waters where the zombyres are faster and more at home. Such sailors pulled overboard are often times held under the water and drowned.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Ahlinni","cr":4,"xp":1200,"alignment":"CN","size":"Medium","type":"magical beast","init":1,"senses":[{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+1 Dex, +6 natural","hp":30,"hd":"4d10+8","hdvalue":4,"hp_mods":["fast healing 2"],"fort":6,"ref":5,"will":1,"resists":[{"type":"sonic","value":10}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":40},{"type":"climb","speed":20}],"melee":"bite +7 (1d8+2)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"breath weapon","details":"20-foot cone of gas, fall prone and hideous laughter 1d3 rounds [CL 4th], Fortitude DC 13 negates, usable every 3 rounds"},{"type":"impale"}],"strength":15,"dexterity":13,"constitution":14,"intelligence":6,"wisdom":10,"charisma":10,"baseatk":4,"cmb":6,"cmd":17,"feats":[{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":5},{"skill":"Climb","value":14},{"skill":"Perception","value":7},{"skill":"Stealth","value":5,"special":"+15 in trees"}],"languages":["birdsong"],"environments":["cold forests","cold marshes","temperate forests","temperate marshes"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","flock (4-7)"],"treasure":"no coins; standard goods (gems only); no items","description_visual":"This creature has a long, straight beak-fully half the length of its body-that tapers to a fine point. Wicked claws sprout from its wings.  Its eyes are whitish-pink and its feathers are a mixture of blues, reds, yellows, and greens.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Birdsong","kind":"Su","details":"An ahlinni can freely communicate with any bird or bird-like creature (such as rocs, giant eagles, and giant owls), perfectly mimicking any bird song or vocalization. Communication with non-intelligent bird creatures is on an empathic level, and can only take place if the bird is within 100 ft. of the ahlinni. In these cases, this ability functions as speak with animals (caster level 4th). This ability is always active, but can be negated. The ahlinni can restart it as a free action on its next turn."},{"type":"Impale","kind":"Ex","details":"When an ahlinni charges a foe, it deals double damage on a successful strike with its bite attack."}],"description":"The ahlinni, or cackle bird, is a flightless bird that dwells in the treetops of thick forests or canopies of thick marsh trees. It is covered in greenish feathers of varying hues, and when it lies motionless in its leafy surroundings it is nearly invisible.\n\nCackle birds live in small flocks, and their laughter-like calls can be heard for great distances through the woods they call home. They have an affinity for small sparkly objects, and often line their nests with bits of colored glass or gemstones in order to attract a mate.\n\nAn ahlinni attacks first with its breath weapon and, being cowardly, usually flees the encounter if possible. When on the offensive, an ahlinni leaps from its treetop and attempts to impale its opponent on its beak.","ecology":"","habitat_society":""},{"name":"Algoid","cr":4,"xp":1200,"alignment":"N","size":"Medium","type":"plant","subtypes":["fungus","psionic"],"init":0,"senses":[{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":16,"ac_touch":10,"ac_flat_footed":16,"ac_mods":"+6 natural","hp":37,"hd":"5d8+15","hdvalue":5,"fort":7,"ref":1,"will":7,"dr":"5/bludgeoning and magic","immunities":["electricity","fire","plant traits"],"weaknesses":["vulnerability to water"],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20}],"melee":"2 slams +8 (1d6+4)","space":"5 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","special_attacks":[{"type":"animate trees"},{"type":"mind blast"},{"type":"stun"}],"spell_like_abilities_caster_level":5,"spell_like_abilities_concentration":8,"spell_like_abilities":[{"frequency":"at-will","spells":[{"spell":"detect thoughts"},{"spell":"know location"}]},{"frequency":"1/day","spells":[{"spell":"shocking grasp"}]}],"strength":19,"dexterity":10,"constitution":16,"intelligence":4,"wisdom":10,"charisma":10,"baseatk":3,"cmb":18,"cmd":29,"feats":[{"feat":"Iron Will"},{"feat":"Skill Focus (Perception)"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (slam)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Perception","value":12},{"skill":"Stealth","value":10,"special":"+18 in swamps and forests"}],"languages":["Common (can't speak)"],"environments":["temperate marshes"],"organizations":["solitary","pair","cluster (3-6)"],"treasure":"standard","description_visual":"This stocky, man-sized creature seems to be composed of living algae, dark green and brown in color.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Animate Trees","kind":"Su","details":"An algoid can animate trees within 90 ft. at will, controlling up to two trees at a time. It takes 1 full round for a normal tree to uproot itself. Thereafter, it moves at a speed of 10 ft. and fights using the stats below. Animated trees lose their ability to move if the algoid that animated them is incapacitated or moves out of range. This ability is otherwise similar to liveoak. Animated Tree: CR 4; N Huge plant; Init -1; Senses low-light vision; AC 16 (-1 Dex, +9 natural, -2 size), touch 7, flat-footed 16; hp 37 (5d8+15); Saves Fort +7, Ref +0, Will +2; DR 5/slashing; Immune plant traits; Weaknesses vulnerability to fire; Spd 30 ft.; Atk 2 slams +7 (2d6+5); Space/Reach 15 ft./15 ft.; SA double damage against objects (full attack deals double damage), trample (2d6+7, DC 17); Str 21, Dex 8, Con 17, Int 4, Wis 12, Cha 10; BAB +3; CMB +10; CMD 19; Feats Improved Sunder, Power Attack, Weapon Focus (slam); Skills Perception +5."},{"type":"Mind Blast","kind":"Sp","details":"Once per day, an algoid can use a mind blast in a 60-foot cone. Any creature caught in the cone must succeed on a DC 14 Will save or be stunned for 3d4 rounds. The save DC is Charisma-based and includes a +2 racial bonus."},{"type":"Stun","kind":"Ex","details":"If an algoid scores a critical hit with a slam attack, the opponent struck must succeed on a DC 16 Fortitude save or be stunned for 1d2 rounds. The save DC is Strength-based."},{"type":"Vulnerability to Water","kind":"Ex","details":"A control water spell deals 1d6 points of damage per caster level (maximum 10d6) to an algoid (no save)."}],"description":"The algoid is a living colony of algae that has developed some semblance of intelligence and mobility. It is roughly humanoid in shape and resembles a green humanoid. Algoids make their lairs in marshes and swamps. They are often encountered with other marsh-dwelling sentient plants; though never with shambling mounds (they hate them and usually attack them on sight). The algoid is 7 ft. tall and weighs about 300 pounds. In its natural surroundings, it is nearly invisible until it attacks and uses this to its advantage when prey is nearby. The \"skin\" of an algoid is coarse and rough with a leafy texture. The algoid often lies in wait, partially submerged in water or a bog, until its prey passes nearby. It springs to attack with its powerful fists when its opponents come within range.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Algoid originally appeared in the First Edition Fiend Folio (© TSR/ Wizards of the Coast, 1981) and is used by permission."},{"name":"Amphisbaena","cr":4,"xp":1200,"alignment":"N","size":"Large","type":"magical beast","init":2,"senses":[{"type":"all-around vision"},{"type":"darkvision","modifier":"60 ft."},{"type":"low-light vision"}],"ac":17,"ac_touch":11,"ac_flat_footed":15,"ac_mods":"+2 Dex, +6 natural, -1 size","hp":42,"hd":"5d10+10 (+5)","hdvalue":5,"fort":6,"ref":6,"will":2,"defensive_abilities":["split"],"immunities":["petrification"],"resists":[{"type":"cold","value":10}],"speed":[{"type":"land","speed":20},{"type":"climb","speed":20},{"type":"swim","speed":20}],"melee":"2 bites +8 (1d8+3 plus poison)","space":"10 ft.","reach":"5 ft.","strength":17,"dexterity":15,"constitution":14,"intelligence":2,"wisdom":12,"charisma":2,"baseatk":5,"cmb":9,"cmd":21,"cmd_special":"can't be tripped","feats":[{"feat":"Lunge"},{"feat":"Toughness"},{"feat":"Weapon Focus (bite)"}],"skills":[{"skill":"Acrobatics","value":2,"special":"-2 jump, +6 when balancing"},{"skill":"Climb","value":11},{"skill":"Perception","value":9},{"skill":"Stealth","value":9},{"skill":"Swim","value":11}],"environments":["any underground"],"organizations":["solitary","pack (2-4)"],"treasure":"none","description_visual":"This creature appears as a huge snake with a head at each end of its body. Its scales are blackish-blue with bands of lighter blue fading into its coloration near the middle of its body. Its heads are glossy-black and its eyes are crimson.","source":"Tome of Horrors Complete (3pp)","special_abilities":[{"type":"Poison","kind":"Ex","details":"Bite-injury; save Fort DC 14; frequency 1/round for 4 rounds; effect 1d3 Con; cure 1 save. The save DC is Constitution-based."},{"type":"Split","kind":"Ex","details":"An amphisbaena functions normally even if cut in half. If dealt a critical hit with a slashing weapon, the creature is cut in half but continues to function as two separate creatures, each with half the original amphisbaena's current hit points (rounded down). Once split, an amphisbaena cannot be split again. If left alone for 1 minute, the split amphisbaena can rejoin its two halves and become a single whole creature again (add the two creatures' hit points together). If one of the split creatures is slain, the amphisbaena can regrow the lost portion over the course of a few weeks."}],"description":"The amphisbaena is a giant poisonous snake about 10 ft. long. It is often found lairing near a water source or in dark, damp locations. An amphisbaena moves on land by grasping one of its necks with its other head and rolling across the ground like a hoop. An amphisbaena is an aggressive and territorial creature, attacking any living creatures that wander near its lair. It attacks by biting with its poisonous fangs from both of its heads.","ecology":"","habitat_society":"","credit":"The Amphisbaena originally appeared in the First Edition Monster Manual (© TSR/Wizards of the Coast, 1977) as Snake, Amphisbaena and is used by permission."}];
